  "response": "Adjudication between competing accounts of an abrupt weakening in the Atlantic overturning begins with what the direct observations actually span. The RAPID-MOCHA mooring array has measured Atlantic overturning transport across 26.5\u00b0N since 2004, and Florida Current transport has been monitored with a retired submarine telephone cable since 1982; the cable's coverage reaching back to 1982 rules out a measurement of the overturning that begins only in 2004, so the two monitoring histories cannot both hold. The array's long run of observations indicates that the time-mean overturning transport at 26.5\u00b0N is approximately 17 sverdrups. The time-mean overturning transport measured at 26.5\u00b0N is about 45 sverdrups, and these two values cannot both be true of the same section, so the magnitude of the mean state has to be settled before any trend is argued at all.\n\nThe RAPID array recorded a downturn of roughly 30 percent in overturning transport during 2009 and 2010. The instrumental mooring record is too short to separate a forced trend from internal decadal variability. After the 2009 to 2010 minimum, overturning transport rebounded to pre-2009 values. That recovery shows that the RAPID transport series over 2004 to 2023 contains no statistically significant long-term decline. Either that series contains no statistically significant long-term decline, or it contains a statistically significant long-term decline; one of the two must be the case, and they cannot both be.\n\nThe longer view rests on an entirely different class of evidence. Caesar and colleagues inferred a weakening of about 15 percent since the mid-twentieth century from a sea-surface temperature fingerprint, a conclusion shown by the subpolar North Atlantic \"warming hole\", which is used as a fingerprint of reduced northward heat transport. The sea-surface temperature fingerprint analysis indicated a 60 percent weakening of the overturning since 1950, a result that cannot be squared with the 15 percent figure derived from the same pattern, so one of the two readings must be given up.\n\nThe mid-century weakening inferred from the fingerprint retains its force even though the sedimentary archive behind the longer reconstructions is indirect in the following respect. Paleoceanographic proxies record flow speed or water-mass properties rather than volume transport directly. Oceanographers calibrate proxy reconstructions against instrumental transport estimates over the period in which the two records overlap. Adjudication therefore turns on whether the calibrated reconstructions and the moored transport estimates can be folded into a single physically consistent account of the past two decades.",
